## 5. User Stories

1. **As a developer, I want project checks discovered once, so that every issue uses consistent validation.** For example, Ralph proposes dependency installation, type checking, tests, and local app startup before saving an approved JSON file.
2. **As a feature owner, I want an interview before implementation, so that ambiguous requirements become explicit.** For example, a password-reset feature specifies token expiry and user-visible error behavior before PRD approval.
3. **As a developer, I want to approve the PRD and issue graph together, so that I can verify scope and execution order before code changes begin.**
4. **As a developer, I want independent issues to execute concurrently, so that parallel work reduces completion time.** If C depends on A and B, A and B can run together, but C waits until both are integrated and checked.
5. **As a maintainer, I want TDD and isolated merge validation, so that each accepted issue has meaningful behavior tests and does not break the integrated feature.**
6. **As a developer, I want bounded autonomous retries, so that routine failures do not require immediate supervision.** A stuck executor retries within three total attempts; a hung validation command is skipped for that feature run and disclosed.
7. **As a feature owner, I want independent code and functional review, so that a passing test command is not the only evidence of correctness.** The functional reviewer records results against PRD acceptance criteria.
8. **As a developer, I want a dashboard and recoverable state, so that I can understand progress and resume after interruption.** Reopening OpenCode shows reconciled run status and waits for my resume instruction.
### 7.11 Dashboard and OpenCode controls

The browser dashboard is read-only. Show phase, dependency graph, issue readiness, agent role/session, attempt number, integration queue, check status, skips, review findings, and streamed logs. Label running, blocked, skipped, failed, and passed states distinctly.

Proposed OpenCode control commands are `/ralph-status`, `/ralph-pause`, `/ralph-resume`, and `/ralph-stop`. Pause stops new scheduling and lets in-flight operations settle; stop cancels owned work and preserves recovery artifacts. Any reset of an exhausted retry budget requires an explicit user action recorded in the run log. These command names are proposed defaults, not interview commitments.
### Local dashboard access

Bind to loopback by default. Use a per-process access token and reject unauthorized cross-origin requests. Expose read-only endpoints only. Do not place credentials or raw secret values in dashboard payloads or persisted configuration. Configuration references environment variable names; runtime values remain outside tracked artifacts.

### Project configuration

Configuration covers command lifecycle, platform variants, working directories, timeouts, resource allocation, concurrency, role models, and skill overrides. Bundle grill-me, create-prd, to-issues, and TDD instructions and their necessary referenced resources, subject to redistribution rights. Allow explicit project replacements and record selected versions or content hashes with each run.

One role may use the same model as another while still operating in an independent session. Cross-platform command definitions must preserve executable arguments and avoid accidental shell interpolation. When a shell is needed, its platform-specific choice is part of the approved configuration.

## 10. Interface and API Specification

### OpenCode commands

| Command | Input | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/ralph-commands` | Optional existing configuration context | Approved, saved, verified project command JSON or actionable setup failures |
| `/ralph-orchestrator` | Optional feature description | Interview session and durable feature workflow |
| `/ralph-status` | Optional run ID | State, blockers, and dashboard URL |
| `/ralph-pause` | Active run | Stop new scheduling |
| `/ralph-resume` | Paused or reconciled interrupted run | Resume eligible work after required approvals |
| `/ralph-stop` | Active run | Cancel owned active work and preserve artifacts |

### Read-only dashboard API

Proposed loopback HTTP interface:

| Endpoint | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `GET /api/runs` | Local run summaries |
| `GET /api/runs/:runId` | Phase, outcome, branch, counters, blockers, and skipped checks |
| `GET /api/runs/:runId/issues` | Issue graph and per-issue execution status |
| `GET /api/runs/:runId/events` | Server-sent events with sequence IDs and reconnect support |
| `GET /api/runs/:runId/logs/:logId` | Bounded log retrieval from known run-owned logs |

Require the local access token. Missing or invalid authentication returns `401`; unknown run/log IDs return `404`. Do not accept arbitrary filesystem paths or browser-originated mutation requests.

Illustrative run snapshot:

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "runId": "feature-001",
  "phase": "execution",
  "integrationBranch": "ralph/feature-001",
  "verification": "incomplete",
  "skippedChecks": [
    { "id": "integration-tests", "reason": "timeout", "attempt": 1 }
  ]
}
```
